#### 8. Exception Handling
**Academic name:** Exception Handling (Peruma et al. 2020)
**Affected:** `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` (lines 110–118)

```csharp
// ❌ Manual try/catch instead of framework-provided assertion
try
{
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.Fail("Expected an exception but none was thrown");
}
catch (ValidationException ex)
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

**Note:** This is a capture-and-assert pattern (captures the exception for property assertion) which is functional but clumsy. MSTest has a built-in for this.

**Fix:** Use `Assert.ThrowsException<T>`:

```csharp
var ex = Assert.ThrowsException<ValidationException>(
    () => processor.ProcessOrder(order));

Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
```

**Risk if unfixed:** If a *different* exception type is thrown, the `catch` silently misses it and the test passes (the `catch` only matches `ValidationException`, but any other exception type propagates as a test error — so the risk is moderate, not critical).

